+# R48c — Q6_K mat-vec decode: attribution and the change
+
+**Inherited:** `9ff4644b1` ("feat(updater): preserve fork work across rebases"), worktree
+`C:/ArifiLabs/cache/r48c-q6k-wt`, branch `lane-236/r48c-q6k-matvec-2026-09-14`, clean apart from the
+untracked prompt file. No commit, no branch move, no upstream operation. **Nothing was compiled and
+nothing was run on the GPU** — the measurement chain owns the box.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Read this before paying for anything else: the 35% deficit is mostly a ROUTE comparison
+
+The brief's premise is that Q6_K mat-vec reads at 41.0 GB/s against q5_K 63.8 and q4_K 72.5, a
+"reproducible ~35% deficit", and asks for +20% toward the 63.8 mark. Those three numbers are **not
+produced by the same framework** on this box.
+
+`ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq()` (`ggml/src/ggml-vulkan/ggml-vulkan.cpp`) decides, in this order:
+
+| step | line (inherited SHA) | effect for n=1, k=5120, AMD/RDNA3 |
+|---|---|---|
+| Q6_K early return, Intel-only | `:11724-11727` | **Q6_K → false.** Never enters MMVQ. |
+| AMD `n > 1` block | `:11801` | not reached at n=1 |
+| `if (n > 1) return true` | `:11816` | not reached at n=1 |
+| vendor switch, `VK_VENDOR_ID_AMD`, `k < 2048 → false`, else `default: return true` | `:11841-11850` | **Q4_K → true. Q5_K → true.** |
+
+So at n=1 on the 780M with k = 5120:
+
+| type | shader actually executed at n=1 |
+|---|---|
+| Q4_K | `mul_mat_vecq.comp` — q8_1 **integer dot** |
+| Q5_K | `mul_mat_vecq.comp` — q8_1 **integer dot** |
+| Q6_K | `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp` — **f32 dequant** |
+
+**CHECKED.** This is the same finding shape as the R48 Q5_K report §1, and it has the same
+consequence: the briefed acceptance rule is not satisfiable as written. ">= +20% median GB/s at n=1
+toward the q5_K 63.8 mark" asks an f32-dequant kernel to close a gap whose largest single term is
+that its two comparands are on the integer path and it is not. **No edit to
+`mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp` can move that term.** §6 gives a pairing plan that measures the two terms
+separately instead of conflating them.
+
+A diagnostic escape hatch is included so HQ can settle it in one round: `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_MMVQ=1`
+puts Q6_K on the same route as its siblings. The `mul_mat_vec_q6_k_q8_1_f32` pipeline is **already
+registered unconditionally** (`:7211`), so this reaches built code — no new shader. Default is the
+shipped route; no default moves (F-08).
+
+---
+
+## 2. Cost attribution
+
+Unit: **one thread, one superblock, one row.** 16 threads cooperate on a 256-weight superblock, so
+one thread owns 16 weights. `NUM_ROWS = rm_kq = 2` on this box (`:6860`, raised to 4 only under
+`AMD_GCN`; the 780M is RDNA3). `NUM_COLS = n`.
+
+### 2.1 Loads
+
+| source | before | after (arm 1) | note |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| `ql` — 4 × `u16` (`packed16[ql_offset/2, +1, +16, +17]`) | 4 | 4 | **cannot be widened, §2.3** |
+| `qh` — 2 × `u16` | 2 | 2 | same |
+| `scales` — 4 × `int8` | 4 | 4 | direct-scales arm; unchanged |
+| `d` — 1 × `f16` | 1 | 1 | |
+| **weight subtotal, per row** | **11 (18 B)** | **11** | |
+| `data_b` — 4 × `vec4`, per column | 4 × NUM_COLS **per row** | 4 × NUM_COLS **per superblock** | the change |
+
+| shape | loads before | loads after | delta |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| n=1, NUM_ROWS=2 | 2·11 + 2·4 = **30** | 2·11 + 4 = **26** | **−13.3%** |
+| n=8, NUM_ROWS=2 | 2·11 + 2·32 = **86** | 2·11 + 32 = **54** | **−37.2%** |
+
+### 2.2 ALU (VALU ops, counted from the GLSL)
+
+Per row: bit extract 17 (`ql` 2 shr + 4 and; `qh` 4 and + 3 shift; 4 or), `unpack8`→`vec4`
+conversion 16, `- 32` 16, per column 20 (16 fma dot + 3 fma + 1 mul reduce).
+
+| shape | before | after (arm 1) | delta |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| n=1, NUM_ROWS=2 | 2·(17+16+16+20) = **138** | 2·(17+16+0+24) + 12 = **126** | **−8.7%** |
+| n=3, NUM_ROWS=2 | 2·(17+16+16+60) = **218** | 2·(17+16+3·24) + 36 = **246** | +12.8% |
+| n=8, NUM_ROWS=2 | 2·(17+16+16+160) = **418** | fold would give **546** | **+30.6% — refused** |
+
+**The fold inverts.** Dropping `- 32` removes 16 subtractions per row; paying the offset once per
+sub-block costs 4 fma per row **per column**. Net `NUM_COLS·4 − 16`: a win at `NUM_COLS <= 3`,
+break-even at 4, a loss at 5..8. The fold therefore carries an n-GATE at `NUM_COLS <= 3`, the same
+shape and for the same reason as the S-X8 n-GATE at `mul_mat_vec_sx8.comp:72`. The hoist has no
+crossover and applies at every width. At n=8 the after-column is the **hoist only**: 418 → 418 ALU,
+86 → 54 loads.
+
+### 2.3 The one large structural difference — and why it cannot be fixed here (CHECKED)
+
+`block_q6_K` is **210 bytes** (`ql` 128 + `qh` 64 + `scales` 16 + `d` 2; `types.glsl:452`). Its
+members are `uint8_t` / `int8_t` / `float16_t`, so the struct's std430 base alignment is **2** and
+the array stride is **210** — matching the on-disk block.
+
+Adding any `uint32_t` member to build a `block_q6_K_packed32` raises the struct's base alignment to
+4, so the array stride becomes `roundUp(210, 4) = 212`. **212 ≠ 210**: every block after the first
+would be read at the wrong offset. The member offsets (0 / 128 / 192 / 208) are individually fine;
+it is the *stride* that breaks. Scalar block layout does not rescue it — that relaxes vector
+alignment, not struct-size padding.
+
+The raw-`uint32_t`-alias escape fails too: block `ib` starts at byte `210·ib`, and
+`210·ib mod 4` alternates 0 (even `ib`) / 2 (odd `ib`), so **half of all blocks are not 4-byte
+aligned**. There is no lawful 32-bit load of `ql`/`qh` for this type.
+
+That completes the attribution chain:
+
+- q4_K reads `data_a_packed32[ib].qs[q_offset/4]` — 16 lanes, dwords at 4-byte stride, one
+  fully-coalesced instruction (`mul_mat_vec_q4_k.comp:37-38`).
+- q6_K reads `packed16[ql_offset/2]` and `[+1]` — bytes `4t` and `4t+2` — as two 2-byte gathers
+  the compiler **cannot** merge, because the containing struct is align-2 so 4-byte alignment
+  cannot be proven (`mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:117-118`).
+
+Same weights, ~2× the load instructions, each half as wide. Upstream already names this: the
+comment at `:11724` reads *"q6_k only has 2-byte alignment which makes it somewhat problematic,
+using MMVQ is only a win on Intel."*
+
+**Fixing it requires changing the GGUF block layout, which is out of scope for a kernel lane.**
+
+### 2.4 Candidate verdicts, against the brief's ordering
+
+| brief candidate | verdict | evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| (1) bit extraction, packed-u32 mask/shift | **already in the tree** — both `ql` and `qh` are already decoded 4 weights per u32 by mask | `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:117-134` (inherited `:62-76`) |
+| (1b) fold the `−32` into a per-sub-block term | **real, un-done — DONE here, n-gated at ≤3** | §2.2 |
+| (2) load width / 16-byte vectors | **BLOCKED by the 210-byte block stride** | §2.3 |
+| (3) occupancy, rows/workgroup probe | **NOT done — named as the lead remaining mechanism** | §5 |
+| (4) activation-side hoist across rows | **real, un-done — DONE here** | §2.1 |
+
+**Honest limit: attribution is not closed.** The counted table above explains a load-instruction
+asymmetry of roughly 2×; it does not by itself account for a ~2.6× per-weight time gap, and my own
+ALU count puts q6_K (≈4.4 VALU/weight) and q4_K (≈4.25) at near parity. Closing the remainder
+needs an ISA dump or a hardware profiler, neither of which this phase could run. §1's route finding
+is the largest single term I could establish with evidence; the rest is counted, not measured.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Mechanism of the change
+
+The four `data_b` `vec4` loads of a superblock do not depend on the row, yet they sat inside
+`for n < num_rows`, so they were issued `NUM_ROWS` times for row-independent bytes. Arm 1 loads
+them once per superblock and reuses them.
+
+That is also what makes the fold possible, because the fold needs an activation-only quantity:
+
+```
+sum_l b[l] * (u[l] - 32)   ==   sum_l b[l] * u[l]   -   32 * sum_l b[l]
+```
+
+`sum_l b[l]` is computed once per superblock per column alongside the hoisted loads and reused
+across every row, so 16 subtractions per row collapse to one fma per sub-block per column.
+
+### Ordering change, stated exactly
+
+- **Before:** `sum[k] = Σ_l fma(b[l], (u[l] − 32), sum[k])` — one fma chain in `l` order, the
+  offset inside it.
+- **After:** `sum[k] = Σ_l fma(b[l], u[l], sum[k])` in the **same** `l` order, then one
+  `sum[k] = fma(−32.0, xs[k], sum[k])`, where `xs[k] = (b.x + b.y) + (b.z + b.w)` — pairwise, order
+  pinned by the parentheses (`dot()` would not pin it).
+- The final `temp[j][n] = fma(fma(sum0,sc0, fma(sum1,sc2, fma(sum2,sc4, sum3*sc6))), d, temp[j][n])`
+  is **unchanged, character for character**.
+
+**Results are not bit-identical to arm 0.** This is a cancellation (`rawsum ≈ 32·xsum`), so the
+accuracy question is real — but `FLOAT_TYPE` is `float` for **every** mat-vec pipeline, never
+`float16_t`: `vulkan-shaders-gen.cpp:846` sets `base_dict = {{"FLOAT_TYPE", "float"}, ...}` and the
+mat-vec block at `:848-861` passes that dict unmodified. CHECKED. In fp32 across 4-term sums the
+absolute error is ~1e-7 · 32 · |b|, far inside the `test-backend-ops` MUL_MAT NMSE gate. The f16
+variant is `B_TYPE = float16_t` — the *activation storage* type — which is converted through
+`FLOAT_TYPE` (= `float`) before any arithmetic, exactly as in arm 0.
+
+---
+
