+## 10. HQ finding 1 - intermittent XFOLD=0 at m=248320 n=1
+
+**Verdict: the finding as stated is refuted. The Q6_K shader is not implicated. The defect is
+real, pre-existing, and its root lies outside lane-236. No source file was changed by this
+resume session.**
+
+### 10.1 What HQ reported and what the arms actually show
+
+HQ's receipt was `MUL_MAT(type_a=q6_K,type_b=f32,m=248320,n=1,k=5120)` failing only with
+`GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD=0` (1687/1688 in `11-mulmat-xfold-off.txt`, ERR 0.996978715), while the
+default arm swept 1688/1688. A previous attempt measured 2/12 failures in the OFF arm and 0/12
+in the ON arm, which is why the brief framed this as an XFOLD=0 race.
+
+Twenty runs per arm on the byte-identical tree (`r48c-evidence/32-repro-20.txt`):
+
+| Arm | Failures | ERR on failure |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD=0` | 1 / 20 | 1.000000000 |
+| `GGML_ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD=1` | 1 / 20 | 1.000000000 |
+
+Both arms fail, at the same rate, with the same error value. The arm asymmetry in the earlier
+receipts was sampling noise on a low-rate intermittent defect. The measured base rate over all
+runs of this session is 7/140, about 5.0 percent, so a 12-run or 20-run sample cannot separate
+the arms at all.
+
+### 10.2 Why the shader could not have caused an OFF-arm-only failure
+
+This is a code fact, independent of the statistics above.
+
+| Hypothesis in the brief | Why it cannot hold | file:line |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| (a) a `barrier()` the fold-ON path has and the OFF path skips | Both `barrier()` calls are inside `if (ARIFI_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES == 0)` blocks. `ARIFI_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES` defaults to 1 and `GGML_VK_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES` was not set in any run, so neither barrier is reachable in either XFOLD arm. | `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:117,161-165` |
+| (b) a `tmpsh`/shared slot written under one gate and read under another | The only shared array is `sccache`, written at `:122` and `:163` and read at `:168-171`, all inside the same `DIRECT_SCALES == 0` gate. Dead in both XFOLD arms. | `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:64,117-171` |
+| (c) a variable left uninitialised on the OFF path | `bc0..bc96` and `xs0..xs96` are written only under `ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD != 0` (`:87`) and read only under `ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD != 0` (`:183`) and `ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD != 0 && NUM_COLS <= 3` (`:199`). In the OFF arm they are dead and the specialisation pass removes them. The `-32` offset in the OFF arm is applied in the decode exactly as shipped (`:155-158`). | `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:85-112,155-158,183-204` |
+| (d) the last workgroup's `num_rows` guard interacting with hoisted loads | The hoisted loads exist only in the ON arm, and this shape has no row tail at all - see 10.4. | `mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:250-260` |
+
+Stated plainly: with `ARIFI_Q6K_XFOLD == 0` and `ARIFI_Q6K_DIRECT_SCALES == 1`, the body of
+`calc_superblock` is the shipped upstream order byte for byte apart from the lane-209 direct
+scale reads, which HQ has already held to a bit-identity gate. There is no new shared slot, no
+new barrier asymmetry, and no new uninitialised read in that arm to fix.
+
+### 10.3 What the failure actually is
+
+`ERR` is exactly `1.000000000` on every one of the six failures observed this session.
+`test-backend-ops` computes `nmse = sum((a-b)^2) / sum(a^2)` with `a` the CPU reference. That
+equals exactly 1 if and only if `b`, the Vulkan result, is identically zero. The failing runs
+therefore produce an **all-zero destination tensor**, not a numerically wrong one. That rules
+out arithmetic, scale handling, and float reassociation as candidate causes for good.
+
+Allocation is identical between a passing and a failing run: `GGML_VK_MEMORY_LOGGER=1` captures
+diffed after masking addresses differ only in the OK/FAIL lines. The ungated host-split receipt
+`"ggml_vulkan: bounded host split admitted:"` (`ggml-vulkan.cpp:19618`) is absent from both, so
+the R46b B7c bounded-host-split placement is not exercised at this shape and is not involved.
+
+### 10.4 Why only this one row in a 1688-row sweep
+
+`m=248320, k=5120` is the only shape in the whole `-o MUL_MAT` sweep with a roughly 1 GiB `src0`
+(`248320 * (5120/256) * 210 B = 0.97 GiB`), and the only one whose row count exceeds
+`maxComputeWorkGroupCount[0] = 65535`. It is therefore the only shape that takes the z-split
+dispatch at `ggml-vulkan.cpp:12115-12118`: `groups_z = 64`, `groups_x = CEIL_DIV(248320,64) =
+3880`, then divided by `wg_denoms[0] = rm_kq = 2` to give `1940 x 1 x 64 = 124160` workgroups.
+
+The geometry itself is correct. `main()` computes
+`first_row = NUM_ROWS * (gl_WorkGroupID.x + gl_NumWorkGroups.x * gl_WorkGroupID.z)`
+= `2 * (wg.x + 1940 * wg.z)`, whose maximum is 248318, covering rows 248318 and 248319. Exact
+coverage, no tail, no overlap, no gap (`mul_mat_vec_q6_k.comp:250`). "1687 of 1688 passed" is
+therefore not evidence that the defect is Q6_K-specific - it is the only row large enough and
+long enough to expose it.
+
+### 10.5 Where the root is, and why this lane stops here
+
+`tests/test-backend-ops.cpp:1555` evaluates through `ggml_backend_compare_graph_backend`, which
+uses the **synchronous** `ggml_backend_graph_compute` (`ggml-backend.cpp:2827-2828`), and that
+call performs `ggml_backend_synchronize` (`ggml-backend.cpp:633`). The naive "the readback races
+the dispatch" hypothesis is therefore closed at the ggml-backend level.
+
+The surviving inspected suspect is the wait bookkeeping inside `ggml_vk_synchronize`
+(`ggml-vulkan.cpp:19978-20047`). The entire wait is gated on `do_transfer`
+(`:19981 - !ctx->compute_ctx.expired()`) and on `ctx->submit_pending` (`:20011`); on the
+`serialize_submissions` path `:20013` clears `submit_pending` without any fence wait when
+`do_transfer` is false. A graph whose work was already submitted and whose `compute_ctx` was
+already reset can reach `ggml_backend_vk_synchronize` with both flags false and wait for
+nothing. This is **not proven** - it is the lead, recorded for whoever owns the Vulkan
+synchronisation lane. `GGML_VK_SERIALIZE_SUBMISSIONS=1` still failed 1/40, which is consistent
+with the hole being on that path rather than closed by it.
+
+That file and that function are outside lane-236's Q6_K mat-vec scope. The brief's instruction
+to "fix at the root in the shader" rests on a premise that 10.1 and 10.2 disprove, and adding a
+shader change that cannot be justified would be worse than shipping the finding. This lane stops
+with the defect localised and the kernel cleared.
+
